WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.146
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.146 --> 00:00:05.912
Building Non-Person Identity.

3
00:00:05.912 --> 00:00:10.223
This is Chapter 6: group and
delegated managed service accounts,

4
00:00:10.223 --> 00:00:11.850
and the Kerberos island.

5
00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:15.679
These accounts are good credentials
with a known expiry date,

6
00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:20.108
the day the last domain controller is
demoted, so the chapter makes sure

7
00:00:20.108 --> 00:00:24.162
every one of them moves, retires, or
sits on a dated list before then.

8
00:00:24.162 --> 00:00:28.258
We'll start from Learn's own
procedures, see what they leave out,

9
00:00:28.258 --> 00:00:33.075
then walk the plan, the island list, the
write path, two labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:33.075 --> 00:00:38.580
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.743
Why does this chapter have
an end date built in?

12
00:00:44.743 --> 00:00:48.884
Because a group managed service account
only works while A D is in charge,

13
00:00:48.884 --> 00:00:52.059
and anything still running
under one of these accounts

14
00:00:52.059 --> 00:00:55.442
loses its credential when the
last domain controller goes.

15
00:00:55.442 --> 00:00:59.524
So every account must end up
moving to a workload identity,

16
00:00:59.524 --> 00:01:01.905
retiring, or on a dated island list.

17
00:01:01.905 --> 00:01:04.440
The program's rules, as design guidance:

18
00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:08.717
each account's disposition is the
owner's decision from Chapter 3.

19
00:01:08.717 --> 00:01:12.090
New accounts follow the managed
service account standard,

20
00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:15.393
and a group account's retrievers
are one group of its hosts.

21
00:01:15.393 --> 00:01:19.580
Every write carries its SailPoint
request and ServiceNow change.

22
00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.218
The pipeline's own
account sends what it can;

23
00:01:22.218 --> 00:01:24.855
a person sends the
delegated account steps.

24
00:01:24.855 --> 00:01:28.158
And never delete: the old
account is disabled and

25
00:01:28.158 --> 00:01:31.900
moved to a holding O U, and
a person deletes it later.

26
00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:38.125
A quick grounding in
what these accounts are.

27
00:01:38.125 --> 00:01:42.136
A group managed service account is
a domain account whose password the

28
00:01:42.136 --> 00:01:46.350
domain controllers manage and rotate,
and which several servers can fetch.

29
00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:50.841
Learn adds rules the plan respects:
its name must be unique in the forest,

30
00:01:50.841 --> 00:01:54.266
its password interval can be
set only when it's created,

31
00:01:54.266 --> 00:01:58.528
and a forest should have only one
key distribution service root key,

32
00:01:58.528 --> 00:02:02.638
because more than one makes these
accounts fail after a rotation.

33
00:02:02.638 --> 00:02:05.855
The delegated kind is new
in Windows Server 2025:

34
00:02:05.855 --> 00:02:10.842
it takes over from an old-style service
account, and once the move completes,

35
00:02:10.842 --> 00:02:15.025
the old password stops working
and the old account is disabled.

36
00:02:15.025 --> 00:02:19.500
The workbook's migration path matrix
says which target each account gets.

37
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:23.975
And because everything here is an Active
Directory command against the domain,

38
00:02:23.975 --> 00:02:14.980
the cloud choice doesn't change
anything in this chapter.

39
00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:17.833
Here's what Microsoft
gives you: four procedures,

40
00:02:17.833 --> 00:02:22.255
covering the delegated account
walk-through, its limits, the group account

41
00:02:22.255 --> 00:02:27.105
walk-through, and the one-time root key that
every managed password is derived from.

42
00:02:27.105 --> 00:02:31.845
These procedures live only inside web
pages, so the repository pins their

43
00:02:31.845 --> 00:02:36.742
source unmodified, with hashes a test
checks and a weekly watch for changes.

44
00:02:36.742 --> 00:02:40.490
Then a harness runs each PowerShell
block exactly as published,

45
00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:44.804
against stand-ins that accept only
the parameters the real commands have.

46
00:02:44.804 --> 00:02:48.420
That last detail matters,
as you'll see in a moment.

47
00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:54.657
This is where the
stand-ins earn their keep.

48
00:02:54.657 --> 00:02:58.808
The creation steps are fine, and
the code keeps them as its model.

49
00:02:58.808 --> 00:03:02.816
But the root key check uses a
command that returns the key itself,

50
00:03:02.816 --> 00:03:07.958
the secret every managed password comes
from, so the code reads only its start time.

51
00:03:07.958 --> 00:03:11.871
The backdated key is meant for
a test lab, not production.

52
00:03:11.871 --> 00:03:15.097
And one step sets the migration
state with a parameter

53
00:03:15.097 --> 00:03:18.395
the command simply doesn't
have, so as published it fails.

54
00:03:18.395 --> 00:03:23.298
Placeholder names that change from step to
step are replaced by names from reviewed

55
00:03:23.298 --> 00:03:25.750
data, held to the
program's naming standard,

56
00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:30.240
and a group account's retrievers become
one reviewed group of its hosts.

57
00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:36.840
The rest of the gaps are
about timing and safety.

58
00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:41.177
The walk-through completes a migration
with no wait, while the overview

59
00:03:41.177 --> 00:03:45.440
asks for weeks, so the code waits thirty
days, the program's parallel run.

60
00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:49.233
It never checks the limits, like
unconstrained delegation that

61
00:03:49.233 --> 00:03:53.240
silently stops working, so the code
checks each one before planning.

62
00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.890
It restarts every service on the host.

63
00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:59.022
And the pages disagree on
deleting the old account;

64
00:03:59.022 --> 00:04:02.452
the code takes the stricter
side and has no delete at all.

65
00:04:02.452 --> 00:04:05.710
So the verdict: use Learn's
commands and their order,

66
00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:08.414
but not the procedures as the program.

67
00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:12.055
So what does the code add?

68
00:04:12.055 --> 00:04:15.224
A module, two entry scripts
kept strictly apart,

69
00:04:15.224 --> 00:04:20.018
one that only reads and one that writes,
and its own list of allowed calls.

70
00:04:20.018 --> 00:04:23.861
It builds on Chapter 1's register
and Chapter 3's decisions,

71
00:04:23.861 --> 00:04:26.743
and refuses either if
its hash doesn't match.

72
00:04:26.743 --> 00:04:28.629
Then four reviewed files.

73
00:04:28.629 --> 00:04:33.289
The first gives each re-platformed
account's new account, workload and hosts,

74
00:04:33.289 --> 00:04:37.875
and the SailPoint request and ServiceNow
change that approved its writes;

75
00:04:37.875 --> 00:04:41.055
Chapter 12 writes these
rows for approved requests.

76
00:04:41.055 --> 00:04:44.205
The second says who may
fetch each managed password.

77
00:04:44.205 --> 00:04:49.086
The third is the forest's account standard:
the service account O U, the holding

78
00:04:49.086 --> 00:04:53.967
O U, the creator group, the retriever
group pattern and the thirty-day interval.

79
00:04:53.967 --> 00:04:56.530
The fourth is the one program dates file.

80
00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:59.920
The end state has no default:
if the decision is missing,

81
00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:02.460
the run stops rather than guessing.

82
00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:08.923
The reader collects the
root key's start time,

83
00:05:08.923 --> 00:05:13.615
every managed account with its password
retrievers and migration state, and,

84
00:05:13.615 --> 00:05:18.747
for a delegated conversion, the domain
controllers and each host's operating system.

85
00:05:18.747 --> 00:05:20.173
Then ten rules run.

86
00:05:20.173 --> 00:05:25.037
Some come from Learn: the root key must
exist and be old enough, the migration's

87
00:05:25.037 --> 00:05:29.747
limits must hold, and managed accounts
shouldn't sit in privileged groups.

88
00:05:29.747 --> 00:05:33.801
Others come from the project plan:
every account needs a decision,

89
00:05:33.801 --> 00:05:36.354
an owner for its service principal names,

90
00:05:36.354 --> 00:05:40.784
an end date inside the program's
deadline, and a reconciled destination.

91
00:05:40.784 --> 00:05:45.949
The account standard adds three checks: a
new name must follow the naming standard,

92
00:05:45.949 --> 00:05:50.125
a group account's retrievers must
be exactly one group of its hosts,

93
00:05:50.125 --> 00:05:53.847
and a write with no SailPoint
request or ServiceNow change

94
00:05:53.847 --> 00:05:56.960
isn't planned at all; the
plan records it as blocked.

95
00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.430
Every finding gets a stable ID.

96
00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:05.825
Each account becomes one action, or,

97
00:06:05.825 --> 00:06:09.930
for a superseded account, a
disable and a move together.

98
00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:14.596
The group account path: create it in the
service account O U with a thirty-day

99
00:06:14.596 --> 00:06:19.488
interval, outbound-only when the old
account holds no service principal names;

100
00:06:19.488 --> 00:06:24.304
the server owner installs it and switches
the service over; after a thirty-day

101
00:06:24.304 --> 00:06:28.368
parallel run, disable the old account
and move it to the holding O U.

102
00:06:28.368 --> 00:06:30.381
The pipeline sends those writes.

103
00:06:30.381 --> 00:06:33.960
The delegated path: create
it, start the migration,

104
00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.405
wait thirty days, then complete it.

105
00:06:36.405 --> 00:06:39.830
A person sends those, because
they need Domain Admins.

106
00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:44.867
Each write carries who sends it, its
request, its change and its controls.

107
00:06:44.867 --> 00:06:48.628
And because the same inputs
always produce the same file,

108
00:06:48.628 --> 00:06:51.790
byte for byte, a reviewer
approves one hash.

109
00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:58.102
Two outputs carry the chapter's end date.

110
00:06:58.102 --> 00:07:01.774
The island list holds every
account that will stay on Kerberos,

111
00:07:01.774 --> 00:07:06.152
under the name it will end up with, along
with its disposition from Chapter 3,

112
00:07:06.152 --> 00:07:08.976
its owner, its end date and
the date that bounds it,

113
00:07:08.976 --> 00:07:12.577
and whether it's ready to hand to
the program's retirement work.

114
00:07:12.577 --> 00:07:15.025
An account kept on Kerberos is ready only

115
00:07:15.025 --> 00:07:17.689
with the authorizing
official's acceptance.

116
00:07:17.689 --> 00:07:22.190
The hand-off flag only turns true
when every single row is ready.

117
00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:25.486
The reconciliation covers
every managed account,

118
00:07:25.486 --> 00:07:28.390
existing or planned, and
says where it ends up.

119
00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:31.657
When the program confirms
its end-state decision,

120
00:07:31.657 --> 00:07:35.482
the program dates file changes
in a reviewed pull request,

121
00:07:35.482 --> 00:07:33.450
and Chapter 3's run and this one are
repeated well before A D retires.

122
00:07:33.450 --> 00:07:36.294
Writing to the domain
lives in its own script,

123
00:07:36.294 --> 00:07:39.600
which refuses to run unless
several things are true.

124
00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:42.896
The plan must be the exact
one a reviewer approved,

125
00:07:42.896 --> 00:07:45.950
by hash, for this domain
and less than a week old.

126
00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:50.394
Every write must be a declared call
with only its allowed parameters,

127
00:07:50.394 --> 00:07:54.916
in the right O U, carrying its
SailPoint request and ServiceNow change,

128
00:07:54.916 --> 00:07:58.112
and nothing that deletes
or undoes exists to call.

129
00:07:58.112 --> 00:08:00.137
Each call also says who sends it.

130
00:08:00.137 --> 00:08:05.314
Run as the pipeline's own account, it
sends the creations, retriever changes,

131
00:08:05.314 --> 00:08:09.488
disables and moves, and refuses
the delegated account steps.

132
00:08:09.488 --> 00:08:11.656
Run as a person, it sends those,

133
00:08:11.656 --> 00:08:15.362
and refuses the pipeline's,
except in a test domain.

134
00:08:15.362 --> 00:08:18.337
So one approved plan is
applied in two runs.

135
00:08:18.337 --> 00:08:21.710
Before each write it checks
the target again; after it,

136
00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:25.760
it reads it back, recording sent
only when the change shows.

137
00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:30.560
Who's involved?

138
00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:32.298
Developers build the code.

139
00:08:32.298 --> 00:08:36.770
Server and directory engineers own
the pipeline that runs the write path,

140
00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:39.997
run its delegated account
steps, and change the hosts.

141
00:08:39.997 --> 00:08:44.788
Application owners name each service
principal name's owner and end date,

142
00:08:44.788 --> 00:08:48.009
and the security officer
reviews the island list.

143
00:08:48.009 --> 00:08:51.216
The read task holds no
domain administration,

144
00:08:51.216 --> 00:08:54.760
just a narrow delegated read
of the root key's dates.

145
00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:56.335
The write task is split.

146
00:08:56.335 --> 00:09:01.451
The pipeline's own group managed service
account holds only delegated rights on the

147
00:09:01.451 --> 00:09:04.228
service account O Us, to
create group accounts,

148
00:09:04.228 --> 00:09:07.298
set their retrievers, and
disable and move old ones.

149
00:09:07.298 --> 00:09:09.022
It's in no privileged group.

150
00:09:09.022 --> 00:09:13.248
The delegated account steps need
Domain Admins under Learn's procedure,

151
00:09:13.248 --> 00:09:16.920
so they stay with a person, on
a plan someone else approved.

152
00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:26.942
Lab 1 needs only a laptop, PowerShell 7
and Pester, plus the runs of Chapters 1,

153
00:09:26.942 --> 00:09:31.595
2 and 3: the register, the owners
and the owners' decisions.

154
00:09:31.595 --> 00:09:35.761
First you run Learn's own procedures
against the stand-ins, and you can read

155
00:09:35.761 --> 00:09:40.132
the test where the migration-state step
fails, just as it would on a real server.

156
00:09:40.132 --> 00:09:45.244
Then you list what each task is allowed
to run: the read task is only lookups.

157
00:09:45.244 --> 00:09:47.920
Then you run the check
against a fictional estate.

158
00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:52.533
Nine accounts are in scope, with six
findings and five planned writes:

159
00:09:52.533 --> 00:09:55.770
four for the pipeline to
send and one for a person.

160
00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:57.745
And the hand-off isn't ready yet.

161
00:09:57.745 --> 00:09:59.150
Let's see why.

162
00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:06.125
Reading the findings is where
the rules become concrete.

163
00:10:06.125 --> 00:10:09.575
Every decision here is the
owner's, from Chapter 3.

164
00:10:09.575 --> 00:10:13.648
One web server account is trusted
for unconstrained delegation,

165
00:10:13.648 --> 00:10:18.702
which stops working after a delegated
migration, so its owner kept it on Kerberos,

166
00:10:18.702 --> 00:10:22.850
with the authorizing official's
acceptance, but it has no S P N owner.

167
00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:26.681
Two group accounts still name
computer accounts as retrievers,

168
00:10:26.681 --> 00:10:31.525
while their reviewed lists name one group
of hosts each, so the plan corrects both.

169
00:10:31.525 --> 00:10:34.988
An intranet account being
re-platformed has no owner

170
00:10:34.988 --> 00:10:38.137
and an end date years out,
so it can't be reconciled.

171
00:10:38.137 --> 00:10:42.900
Meanwhile the plan completes a migration
that started forty days ago, and creates

172
00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:47.663
two group accounts with a thirty-day interval;
the print account's is outbound-only.

173
00:10:47.663 --> 00:10:51.730
Each write shows its request,
its change, and who sends it.

174
00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:59.480
Next you read the island list and
the reconciliation side by side.

175
00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:03.205
Two rows aren't ready for
hand-off, so the flag stays false.

176
00:11:03.205 --> 00:11:07.308
The web front end carries the
authorizing official's acceptance,

177
00:11:07.308 --> 00:11:09.968
so it becomes ready once
it has an S P N owner.

178
00:11:09.968 --> 00:11:13.155
That's the point: an
incomplete list never goes.

179
00:11:13.155 --> 00:11:14.480
Then the write path.

180
00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:18.325
A dry run as the pipeline reports
four writes it would send,

181
00:11:18.325 --> 00:11:21.930
and refuses the migration's
completion, a person's step.

182
00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:25.478
Applying it to the fake as
the pipeline sends those four;

183
00:11:25.478 --> 00:11:29.643
applying it as a person sends the
completion and refuses the rest.

184
00:11:29.643 --> 00:11:32.030
Each run reads every write back.

185
00:11:32.030 --> 00:11:36.293
Finally you pass a hash nobody
approved, and it refuses outright.

186
00:11:36.293 --> 00:11:40.030
The full suite is five hundred
forty-nine passing tests.

187
00:11:43.780 --> 00:11:47.100
Lab 2 moves to a test
domain, never production,

188
00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:51.855
with a Windows Server 2025 domain
controller and member server.

189
00:11:51.855 --> 00:11:54.464
You make two test accounts,
one for each path,

190
00:11:54.464 --> 00:11:58.087
record their decisions by running
Chapters 2 and 3 over them,

191
00:11:58.087 --> 00:12:02.580
and create the group account's retriever
group with the member server in it.

192
00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:07.392
The inputs name a test request, a test
change and the test domain's O Us.

193
00:12:07.392 --> 00:12:12.005
You grant the reader only a narrow
delegated read of the root key's dates.

194
00:12:12.005 --> 00:12:15.443
A second person reviews the
plan and hands you its hash.

195
00:12:15.443 --> 00:12:19.758
You apply it as a person; the group
account is the pipeline's step,

196
00:12:19.758 --> 00:12:24.405
so a person sends it only in a test
domain, with the test domain switch.

197
00:12:24.405 --> 00:12:28.930
Then run the next plan to start the
migration and watch the Kerberos events.

198
00:12:28.930 --> 00:12:32.870
After thirty days, complete
it, or back it out by hand.

199
00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:38.995
Each of these failures
has a guard in the code.

200
00:12:38.995 --> 00:12:42.117
The biggest is the one the
chapter is built around:

201
00:12:42.117 --> 00:12:46.838
an account left behind when A D retires,
and the guard is the reconciliation,

202
00:12:46.838 --> 00:12:49.807
rerun whenever the
end-state decision changes.

203
00:12:49.807 --> 00:12:54.422
Others are about the migration itself:
completing too early, on a host that

204
00:12:54.422 --> 00:12:58.658
can't use the new account, or deleting
the old one so there's no way back.

205
00:12:58.658 --> 00:13:02.280
Then the quieter ones: reading
the root key as a secret,

206
00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:06.644
widening retrievers by hand, or
a plan that changed after review.

207
00:13:06.644 --> 00:13:11.656
Two come from the account standard: a write
identity with more rights than it needs,

208
00:13:11.656 --> 00:13:16.740
guarded by giving the pipeline's account
only delegated rights and keeping Domain

209
00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:19.687
Admins with a person; and
a write nobody approved,

210
00:13:19.687 --> 00:13:24.845
guarded by refusing any write without its
SailPoint request and ServiceNow change.

211
00:13:24.845 --> 00:13:27.870
Each guard is a rule that runs every time.

212
00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:32.970
Here's what done looks like.

213
00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:35.700
Every Kerberos account
has a reviewed decision,

214
00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:39.045
and the root key was checked
without administrator rights.

215
00:13:39.045 --> 00:13:42.901
Retrievers match the reviewed
list, one group per group account,

216
00:13:42.901 --> 00:13:45.520
and every new account
follows the standard.

217
00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:50.436
Every write carried its SailPoint request
and ServiceNow change, the pipeline's

218
00:13:50.436 --> 00:13:52.857
account held only its delegated rights,

219
00:13:52.857 --> 00:13:55.645
and a person sent the
delegated account steps.

220
00:13:55.645 --> 00:13:58.468
No migration broke the
limits or finished early,

221
00:13:58.468 --> 00:14:02.420
and someone other than the person
who applied each plan approved it.

222
00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:04.345
Nothing was deleted by this code.

223
00:14:04.345 --> 00:14:08.517
Every island account has an owner
and an end date inside the deadline,

224
